content.text:
I found two independent commits that both added the identical, unguarded `getSession()` method on separate branch lines later merged into history.

## Bug-Introducing Commits
BIC: 8470942c3a29fe34f953ada8dc251ffbe62397bd, e7acb19731806c6c08ce5c7fc36e8955596d954b

## Reasoning

**(1) Investigation:** The fix adds a null check (`if (getRelatedCourses() == null) return null;`) at the top of `CourseEvent.getSession()`. I traced the full history of `CourseEvent.java` with `git log --follow` to find when `getSession()` was first introduced, since that's the method being patched.

**(2) The introducing change:** Two commits, `8470942c3` (2018-05-15 09:39) and `e7acb19731` (2018-05-15 12:29), each add the exact same code:
```java
public Session getSession() {
    for (RelatedCourseInfo rc: getRelatedCourses())
        return rc.getCourse().getInstructionalOffering().getSession();
    return null;
}
```
Neither checks whether `getRelatedCourses()` is `null` before iterating — this is precisely the defect the fix commit addresses. `getRelatedCourses()` is backed by a Hibernate-mapped `Set<RelatedCourseInfo>` (`BaseCourseEvent.iRelatedCourses`) that is only initialized lazily via `addTorelatedCourses(...)` or by Hibernate on load; a transient/newly-constructed `CourseEvent` (e.g. during data import, before the related-courses collection is populated) has this field as `null`, so the `for` loop throws an NPE.

**(3) Why two commits:** `git log --follow` showed both commits with byte-identical diffs. I confirmed neither is an ancestor of the other (`git merge-base --is-ancestor` returns false both ways), and their parent commits (`b21379e62`, `f15771f91`) are also unrelated by ancestry. This indicates the same change was applied independently on two divergent branches (consistent with UniTime's practice of maintaining parallel release branches), which were later merged into the branch that became `HEAD`. Both independently introduced the same unguarded loop, so both are legitimate origins of the bug prior to the merge; the single fix commit (`8957338e5`) then patches the merged/unified file.

I'm confident in the root cause (missing null guard added in these two commits matches the fix exactly). The only uncertainty is attribution nuance: since both commits are cherry-picks of the identical change on separate branches, you could reasonably say "the bug was introduced once logically" even though it shows up twice in the DAG — I've listed both since each independently carried the defect into its respective branch history.

